{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "dept_decline_rate (expense decline rate, varying across departments)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "is_declined",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Row-level indicator of the decline outcome; it is the raw outcome event, not a treatment or confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "has_source_id",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A candidate cause surfaced from the data (whether an expense has a source_id) that could plausibly drive decline likelihood; not named in the query, so it functions as a possible treatment/explanatory factor."
    },
    {
      "name": "dept_total_count",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Department volume can plausibly relate to both processing behavior/treatment prevalence and decline rate; but here it is essentially the denominator used to compute the outcome rate, making it more mechanical than a genuine common cause."
    },
    {
      "name": "dept_declined_count",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "This is the numerator of the decline rate — a direct component of the outcome, not an independent cause or confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "dept_decline_rate",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "This is the focus outcome itself (departmental decline rate), directly named/implied by the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "processing_days",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Processing time is plausibly on the causal path (a department's practices affect processing time, which affects decline decisions); treating it as a confounder would be a mistake — it is more naturally a mediator."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["has_source_id"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "dept_total_count", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.3}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.2,
  "rationale": "The added columns are overwhelmingly mechanical decompositions of the outcome itself: is_declined, dept_declined_count, dept_decline_rate are the numerator/denominator/rate that define the very quantity the query asks about, and do not set up a causal estimation. A plausible treatment (has_source_id) is surfaced from the data, satisfying part (a) weakly. However, the causal claim requires genuine CONFOUNDERS — common causes of both a treatment and the outcome — surfaced from the data. None qualify: dept_total_count is essentially the rate denominator rather than a true common cause, and processing_days is best read as a mediator on the causal path, not a confounder. There are no variables (e.g., expense category mix, amount, user, requestor seniority) surfaced as adjustment variables that jointly cause a treatment and the decline outcome. Thus the decisive test — surfacing non-query confounders that must be adjusted — is essentially not met."
}
```

